IData Business Portfolio: Business and Software Requirements
Build prompt for the AI agent (Google AI Studio) | Version 2.0 | November 2026
How to use this document: paste everything from Part 1 onward into Google AI Studio as the build prompt. Items marked [Owner decision] already have a default, so the agent never has to stop and ask. Change them before or after the build.
Part 1: Mission and Rules
1. Your role and mission
You are a senior full-stack engineer, UX designer and technical writer. Build IData, a multi-page business website for a data, AI and software delivery company. The site must feel like a premium, trustworthy agency: a striking but short home page, great cards, real animation, a reviews page, a client portal and a professional footer. Deliver a complete project the owner can export, run on his own computer, and deploy.
The finished project must run locally with zero configuration (demo mode with sample data), and must switch to live data as soon as the owner adds his own keys.
2. Business context
IData sells four service areas: AI and agentic systems, data analytics and dashboards, data science and machine learning, and software and web development. Work is delivered by a core team plus vetted freelancers, so the site presents a team and a delivery process, not one person.
• Audiences: international clients (freelance and remote), larger companies and agencies, Nigerian SMEs and startups.
• Goals: get quote requests and booked calls, prove credibility through case studies and reviews, and let clients follow their projects in a portal.
• Tone: corporate and trustworthy. Plain language, confident, no hype, no jargon.
• Positioning line (draft): IData turns business data into decisions through AI, analytics and software, delivered by a trusted team.
3. Rules you must follow
1. Use exactly this stack: React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind CSS + Framer Motion + React Router, Supabase for database, login and file storage, Vercel for hosting, Calendly for booking.
2. Never hardcode text, images, links or lists inside components. All content lives in src/content/, all settings in src/config/site.config.ts.
3. Secrets live only in environment variables. Provide .env.example. Only Supabase's public (anon) key may reach the browser.
4. Demo mode: if environment variables are empty, the site still runs. Forms show a success message and log the data, the portal uses sample data, and a small dev-only banner says demo mode is on.
5. Every spot where the owner must type something carries a // OWNER: comment and is listed in OWNER_GUIDE.md (Section 20).
6. Produce two readmes: a public README.md and an owner-facing OWNER_GUIDE.md.
7. The header stays minimal (Section 6). People move around the site through CTAs, cards and one full-screen menu, not through a long list of header links.
8. Stay free-tier friendly: at most three videos, optimized images, lazy loading, no heavy libraries without a reason.
9. No lorem ipsum. Write realistic placeholder copy and mark it // OWNER: replace.
10. Keep the architecture easy to migrate and modify (Section 5).
4. Technology and what each part does
React only builds what the visitor sees in the browser. There is no custom backend server to maintain. Supabase supplies the database, user login, file storage and security rules. One small serverless function on Vercel sends email notifications. This keeps the project simple, free and easy to move later.
