Architecture principles
• Content layer: pages read content through typed functions (getServices()), never import raw data directly. Today they read src/content; later they can read Supabase without touching the pages.
• Service layer: database.ts defines an interface (createLead, listProjects, submitReview and so on). Supabase is one implementation. Replacing it with another database means writing one new file.
• Feature folders: each feature owns its pages, components and hooks.
• Types first: shared TypeScript types for every data shape, matching the database tables.
• Config over code: colors, fonts, links, emails and feature switches are set in config. Provide feature flags such as enablePortal, enableReviewsSubmission, enableBlog.
• Lazy routes and code splitting so the first load stays small.
6. Navigation model (minimal header)
The owner does not want many header items. Build it like this:
• Header (slim, sticky): logo on the left; on the right a search icon, a Menu button and one accent button, Request a Quote. On desktop show at most three plain links (Services, Work, Reviews). Everything else lives in the menu.
• Menu: a full-screen overlay with large animated links to every page, grouped (Services with its four sub-pages, Company, Client area), a search field, social links and the quote and booking buttons.
• Search: the search icon opens a command palette (also opened with Ctrl+K or Cmd+K) that searches services, case studies, team and FAQs and jumps to the result.
• CTA-first navigation: every page ends with a Next step band of two or three cards pointing to related pages, and service pages link to matching case studies and reviews. Cards and buttons do the navigating, not header links.
• Mobile: a slim bottom bar with Menu, Search and Quote. Menu opens the same overlay.
• Back to top button and visible breadcrumbs on inner pages.
