10. Card design
Cards are the main building block, so make them excellent. Build one Card component with variants:
• Service card: icon in a glowing badge, title, one-line promise, three bullets, Explore CTA, spotlight glow that follows the cursor, slight 3D tilt, border that brightens on hover.
• Case study card: cover image or video thumbnail, client type tag, result headline (one number), tools row, hover reveals a short summary and an Open CTA.
• Review card: quote, star rating, name, role and company, small avatar, a verified badge for approved reviews.
• Team card: photo, name, role, three skill chips, social links on hover.
• Stat card: large animated number, label.
• Next-step card: icon, title, one line, arrow; used at the bottom of every page for navigation.
All cards share rounded corners (16 to 24 pixels), a subtle glass or gradient surface, a one-pixel border and consistent padding. Hover and focus states are required on every card.
11. Animation requirements
The owner does not want basic fade-ins. Build polished, purposeful motion with Framer Motion and CSS. Required:
• Page transitions between routes (short cross-fade with slight slide).
• Hero: animated gradient mesh, word-by-word headline reveal, parallax floating graphic that follows the mouse, animated gradient border on the main button.
• Scroll reveals with staggered children, used on every section, never longer than 600 milliseconds.
• Infinite marquee with two opposing rows, pause on hover, edge fade.
• Spotlight and tilt on cards.
• Animated counters that count up when they scroll into view.
• Menu overlay that opens with a staggered link reveal.
• Command palette that scales in with a soft blur backdrop.
• Scroll progress bar at the top of the page.
• Text and number micro-interactions: underline sweeps on links, arrow nudges on CTAs.
• Skeleton loaders for anything that loads from the database.
Rules: animate only transform and opacity for speed, respect prefers-reduced-motion (turn motion off), keep the page smooth on a mid-range phone, and lazy-load heavy effects. Do not animate everything at once.
12. Reviews page
Build /reviews as a showpiece:
1. Summary header: average rating, number of reviews, and a rating breakdown bar.
2. Featured carousel: large review cards, auto-play, swipe on mobile, arrows and dots, pause on hover.
3. Two opposing marquee rows of short review cards (one-liners) moving continuously.
4. Filter chips: by service, by client type (international, agency, SME).
5. Masonry wall of all approved reviews with a Load more button.
6. Leave a review form: name, company, role, service, rating, text, optional photo. Submissions go to the database as pending and appear only after the owner approves them in the admin area.
7. Closing Next step band: Request a Quote, Book a Call.
Seed reviews come from src/content/reviews.ts (clearly marked as placeholders). Live approved reviews come from the reviews table and are merged in.
13. Footer
A professional, well-designed footer on every page:
• Top band: a short call to action with Request a Quote and Book a Call.
• Four columns: brand block (logo, one-sentence description, social icons), Services (the four links), Company (About, Team, Work, Reviews, Contact), Client area (Portal sign-in, Support email, FAQs).
• Newsletter signup (saves to the database) [Owner decision].
• Bottom bar: copyright, Privacy, Terms, and a back-to-top button.
• Subtle gradient divider and a soft animated glow behind the brand block. Fully responsive: columns stack into expandable sections on mobile.
Part 3: Data, Integrations, Documentation
14. Database design (Supabase, PostgreSQL)
Public marketing content (services, case studies, team, FAQs) stays in src/content so it is fast, free and easy to edit. The database holds only data that changes while the site runs. Write the full design as supabase/schema.sql and supabase/policies.sql, with comments, so the owner can paste them into the Supabase SQL editor.
